not much.

94
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:20,000
It pays for the different services that I have to engage to

95
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,000
keep the blog online and to publish the podcast.

96
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,000
It pays for some of the equipment involved-

97
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
I mean, there's microphones and stuff like that that

98
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:28,000
you need-

99
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:31,000
And on occasion there's enough of a surplus to

100
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:35,000
compensate me in a very small way for some of the time

101
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:37,000
I spend provoking your thoughts.

102
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:41,000
So so in this podcast we're going to in scout

103
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,000
master ship in seven minutes or less, continue with our

104
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
series on four steps to scout advancement.

105
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,000
Last time we had the introduction, this time

106
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:49,000
step number one,

107
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
And then I have a couple of email questions and

108
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,000
answers to share with you.

109
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
So let's get started. shall we scout master ship in

110
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:01,000
seven minutes or less?

111
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,000
Last week we introduced this series about the four

112
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,000
steps to scout advancement,

113
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
And last time around we contextualize advancement

114
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
within all the aims and methods.

115
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,000
This week we're going to talk about step one, and

116
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,000
that is that a scout learns.

117
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Now the subsequent steps are: step two, a scout is

118
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:30,000
tested. step three, a scout is reviewed. and step

119
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,000
four, a scout is recognized.

120
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,000
So how does learning happen in the context of

121
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:35,000
scouting?

122
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:38,000
Well, let me read you this quote from Albert Einstein.

123
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:41,000
He said this: it is in fact nothing short of a

124
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
miracle that the modern methods of instruction

125
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,000
have not entirely strangled the Holy Spirit of

126
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
Inquiry,

127
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,000
For this delicate little plan, aside from

128
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,000
stimulation, stands mainly in need of freedom.

129
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:57,000
Without this, it goes to rack and ruin without

130
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:58,000
fail.

131
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
It can be a very grave mistake to think that the

132
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:05,000
enjoyment of seeing and searching can be

133
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:08,000
promoted by means of coercion and a sense of

134
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:09,000
duty.

135
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,000
And in the Scout Master's Handbook we read

136
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:14,000
this: learning is a natural outcome of

137
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,000
scouting activities.

138
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,000
Instruction in scout skills can come from a

139
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
number of sources.

140
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
Most important are the scouts themselves,

141
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:26,000
sharing what they know with one another and

142
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:29,000
helping each other along the advancement trail.

143
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:33,000
So when we introduce this idea of learning

144
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:37,000
into the context of scouting, we usually

145
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:40,000
think of the more common academic model of

146
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:41,000
learning,

147
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,000
And I identify that model this way:

148
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
The students are passive, the teachers are

149
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:50,000
active and the attainment of a certain

150
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:53,000
standard of performance is paramount,

151
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:56,000
And we need to understand that scouting

152
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:00,000
is not an academic exercise in that sense,

153
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,000
And that's the key,

154
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,000
That's what I really want to get across to you-

155
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:07,000
We can say scouting is not school, and that

156
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:09,000
is half the message.

157
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:12,000
But what we really want to think about is

158
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:15,000
that scouting focuses on what Einstein calls

159
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:18,000
that Holy Spirit of Inquiry.

160
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:22,000
Learning in scouting is experiential,

161
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:24,000
a process of discovery.

162
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:27,000
It's not an academic exercise.

163
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:31,000
Learning is the natural outcome

164
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,000
of scout activities.

165
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:36,000
We don't put the cart before the horse here.

166
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,000
We do what scouts do and they learn

167
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,000
as a result of doing that.

168
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:43,000
And so there's this kind of subtle emphasis

169
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:47,000
that I think we find lacking in a lot of places,

170
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:51,000
that we start with learning stuff

171
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:54,000
and the activities just kind of follow in suit.

172
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:56,000
Well, it's totally the other way around.

173
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:59,000
We start with doing what scouts do

174
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
and they learn as a result.

175
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:04,000
And this process of discovery

176
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:06,000
is one question following another,

177
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:08,000
just like a path of breadcrumbs,

178
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:12,000
until scouts discover what they need to know.

179
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,000
So what's the practical purpose

180
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:18,000
in learning to tie a square knot?

181
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:21,000
Well, it joins two ropes together.

182
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,000
What does a scout gain

183
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
from learning to tie a square knot?

184
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:29,000
On the surface, he learns to join two ropes together

185
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
And that's a great practical skill.

186
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:34,000
But simply learning that practical skill

187
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,000
isn't the point.

188
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,000
Our aim is the process of discovery

189
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:44,000
that enables scouts to lead themselves,

190
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:46,000
and subsequently others,

191
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:49,000
to learning anything that they need to know.

192
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:52,000
Self-leadership is the first step

193
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:53,000
in leadership development.

194
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
You learn to lead yourself

195
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:58,000
and then you're able to lead others.

196
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,000
Scouts discover these things

197
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,000
in the course of doing what scouts do,

198
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:05,000
and then they master them

199
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:08,000
and they begin to share them with their fellow scouts.

200
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:12,000
That's the focus of what scouting is

201
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:15,000
and how it's different from that academic model.

202
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
Now, anybody can teach a scout

203
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,000
to tie a square knot in a few minutes,

204
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:20,000
Left over right, right over left.

205
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:21,000
Now you try it.

206
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:22,000
The scout's passive,

207
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:23,000
I'm active,

208
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:24,000
He watches,

209
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:25,000
Then he tries.

210
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
It's an echo of what he's done in school

211
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:28,000
a thousand times:

212
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:29,000
Listen to the teacher,

213
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
Do what the teacher does.

214
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:32,000
This is efficient,

215
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:33,000
This is effective,

216
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
But I'm going to contend

217
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
that it's not really scouting.

218
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:38,000
Whenever possible-

219
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
and it's almost always possible-

220
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:43,000
we initiate this process of discovery

221
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
In the course of a camping trip.

222
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,000
a scout needs to tie two ropes together.

223
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
How does he make that happen?

224
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
Well, does he have a scout handbook?

225
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
Can he find out how to do this

226
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
by looking in the handbook.

227
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:56,000
Can he find the right page?

228
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:58,000
What does it say there?

229
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,000
Can you learn how to do this?

230
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:01,000
Yes, I can.

231
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:02,000
Can you show it to somebody else?

232
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:03,000
Yes, I can.

233
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:06,000
A whole other field of inquiry opens up

234
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:08,000
when we ask other questions.

235
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,000
What does a square knot symbolize in scouting?

236
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
When would you use a square knot?

237
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
You'd probably use it twice a day

238
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
at a minimum to tie your shoes.

239
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:18,000
What? That's not a square knot?

240
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:19,000
Are you sure?

241
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
Can you see a square knot in there?

242
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
One question follows another,

243
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:26,000
building on this spirit of independent inquiry,

244
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
this spirit of discovery,

245
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:29,000
And pretty soon

246
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
we're out of the picture all together,

247
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:34,000
because the scout knows how to find things out.

248
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:35,000
Learning skills in scouting

249
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
is kind of like a head fake in basketball.

250
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
Do you know what a head fake is?

251
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
That's when a player fakes you out

252
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:42,000
by looking in one direction

253
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:43,000
and moving in another direction.

254
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
In scouting, it looks like

255
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:46,000
what we want to do

256
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
is just learn some practical knowledge and skills,

257
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
but we're really off in another direction.

258
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:53,000
Learning how to learn

259
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
is what we're looking for.

260
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
A skill that far surpasses the simple knowledge

261
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
of joining those two ropes together

262
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:03,000
is the skill of learning how to learn.

263
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
As scouts gain these practical skills,

264
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:08,000
they begin to instruct and lead others,

265
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:11,000
and then we start to reap the other benefits

266
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
of this discovery process.

267
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:17,000
Discovery is not as linear and efficient

268
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
as the academic method,

269
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
but what does it create?

270
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:22,000
At an Eagle border review,

271
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
what's more important to you?

272
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
A scout who remembers how to tie a square knot

273
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:29,000
or a scout who's capable of leading himself

274
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
and others to discover

275
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
and accomplish nearly anything?

276
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:36,000
Ideally, scouts gain achievement towards advancement

277
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
as a natural outcome of what they're doing

278
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:39,000
in their patrol or troop.

279
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:42,000
We don't do requirements,

280
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
We do what scouts do.

281
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
And what do scouts do?

282
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:47,000
They go camping and hiking,

283
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:48,000
They build a fire,

284
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:49,000
They cook over a fire,

285
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:50,000
They build towers and bridges,

286
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:51,000
They play games.

287
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
If scouts are doing what scouts do,

288
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:56,000
they will be completing requirements.

289
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
I for sure learned the hard way

290
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
that nothing drains the spontaneous joy

291
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
and excitement out of scouting

292
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
More than telling scouts something like

293
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
you know now, fellas,

294
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
we're going to work on requirements.

295
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:10,000
They have to put down the fun,

296
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
intriguing, interesting things they're doing

297
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
and stand around listening to somebody

298
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:18,000
tell them how to do requirements.

299
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:21,000
So the way a scout learns

300
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
and the place of learning in scouting

301
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:28,000
creates useful, even important, life skills,

302
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:31,000
But those skills are nothing more

303
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
than a means to a greater end,

304
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:36,000
And that is developing a capable,

305
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:39,000
contributing, decent human being.

306
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
We work towards this by inspiring

307
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
what Einstein called

308
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
that Holy Spirit of Inquiry

309
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
and desire for achievement.

310
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
that is measured by the effort

311
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
that a scout extends,

312
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:54,000
not just by the sum of his performance,

313
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
And we're going to look closely at

314
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
step number two. a scout is tested

315
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
in our next podcast.

316
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
Oh, I'm in love with the man who loves to camp.

317
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
He camps when it's sunny,

318
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:08,000
He camps when it's damp.

319
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
All he needs is a tent, a sleeping roll,

320
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:13,000
a package of hot dogs and a sack of charcoal.

321
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:14,000
He camps in the desert,

322
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:15,000
He camps in the snow.

323
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:18,000
He says: baby, come with me,

324
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:22,000
A snowman,

325
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:25,000
Write me a letter,

326
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:28,000
Send it by name.

327
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,000
Email, that is, folks.

328
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,000
And here's an answer to one of your

329
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:41,000
emails.

330
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
I received two emails since the last podcast

331
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,000
that basically had the same kind of question,

332
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:48,000
So I'm kind of mixing those two emails together.

333
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
On a recent camping weekend

334
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,000
the boys did not help with cooking,

335
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,000
clean up and washing dishes

336
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
as much as they should have.

337
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:57,000
They would begin their tour

338
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
but then begin to vanish to other places

339
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:00,000
to have fun.

340
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
So when they woke up Sunday morning

341
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,000
looking for a hot breakfast of eggs.

342
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,000
the adult leaders said it was not happening

343
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,000
because griddles did not get cleaned

344
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:09,000
the prior night.

345
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
They also littered the campsite with

346
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
wrappers the night before.

347
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:14,000
Of course, the next morning

348
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:15,000
they cleaned up the site,

349
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:16,000
but that's not the point.

350
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:17,000
In the past,

351
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
adults have done all the cooking on trips.

352
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
What suggestions do you have

353
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:22,000
for working this out

354
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,000
and keeping the boys accountable and responsible?

355
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,000
Our troop is a good troop,

356
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
but they're primarily adult-led,

357
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:29,000
not patrol-led.

358
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
I realize Rome wasn't built in today,

359
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
so how can they begin to teach

360
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
the boys what they should have been doing

361
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:36,000
all along?

362
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
What do you do about a situation

363
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:39,000
when boys vanish

364
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:40,000
when there's work to be done?

365
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
How do you start out teaching

366
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
clueless boys how to cook and wash dishes?

367
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:46,000
I think that adults

368
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
need to model what is required

369
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:49,000
and work with them,

370
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
teach them and direct them.

371
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:52,000
We have a great group of adults

372
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
and the troop has approximately 30 boys

373
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,000
and the majority of them are on the younger side.

374
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:59,000
Thanks for any thoughts you may have about this.

375
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
The situation you describe

376
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
is not all that uncommon

377
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
and its cause is

378
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
adults running the troop,

379
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
Plain and simple.

380
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
So I want to get you started thinking

381
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:15,000
about a few basic ideas.

382
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:16,000
First,

383
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
scouting is something

384
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
that scouts do for themselves,

385
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
not something that adults

386
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
present to them.

387
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:25,000
Second,

388
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
scouts can do everything

389
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
for themselves

390
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
Other than, you know,

391
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
a very few administrative functions.

392
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
Scouts can make menus

393
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
and purchase food and cook for

394
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
and clean up after themselves.

395
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
They can plan their activities,

396
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
instruct and prepare those fellow scouts

397
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:44,000
to participate in them.

398
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
They can lead their fellow scouts through activities

399
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
and help their fellow scouts advance and rank.

400
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
The adult role in scouting

401
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
is helping scouts discover

402
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
their leadership abilities

403
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
and helping scouts find the resources

404
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
that they need and getting out of the way

405
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
so that they can do it.

406
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
Let me tell you about my weekend,

407
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:05,000
and I'm telling you about it

408
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:08,000
just to contrast what you've described.

409
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
Well, I'm really proud of my scouts.

410
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
They're not super humans or anything like that.

411
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
They're just like your scouts,

412
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
And last weekend we went backpacking with them.

413
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
We had 17 scouts with a pretty good distribution

414
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
of ages from 11 to 16,

415
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
and we had nine adults along-

416
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
a lot for us on any given weekend,

417
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
But we've got a lot of guys

418
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
who really like to go backpacking.

419
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
The adult role on this outing

420
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
was basically chartering the bus

421
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
that took us there,

422
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
helping the patrol leadership council

423
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
determine what route they would follow

424
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
and accompanying the scouts on the trip.

425
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
Everything else

426
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
was done by the scouts.

427
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
They planned their meetings to prepare themselves

428
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
for backpacking,

429
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
with instruction on the proper gear

430
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
and food that they'd need and things like that,

431
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
And adults did know

432
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
not even a minute's worth of instruction.

433
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
Everything done in those meetings

434
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
was done by the boys.

435
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
Each patrol planned their meals,

436
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
purchased and packed their food,

437
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
prepared it and cleaned up after themselves.

438
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
No adult touched a pot or a pan

439
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
or a stove

440
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
or any of the food the scouts brought.

441
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
We cooked for ourselves.

442
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
We got there Friday night

443
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
and the scouts had a campsite

444
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
from the adults.

445
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
They set up their own tents.

446
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
They camped and hiked

447
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
the whole weekend as patrols,

448
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
each patrol staying together.

449
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
For the most part the scouts hiked

450
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
well in front of the adult group.

451
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
No adults hiked with the scouts

452
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
because the scouts

453
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
looked after themselves.

454
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
We observed and advised

455
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
only if we were asked

456
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
or if a decision on part of the scouts

457
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
would lead to some kind of you know

458
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
situation, which, by the way,

459
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
wasn't really necessary. on that weekend

460
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
I spoke to the senior patrol leader

461
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
for perhaps a total of 20 minutes

462
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
for the whole weekend,

463
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
mostly to check in and to review what was going on.

464
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
So how did things go?

465
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
Well, I could have solved some problems

466
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
for them, but they figured things out in the end.

467
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
They did not do everything

468
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
perfectly, nor did they do everything

469
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
the way that I would have done them

470
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
or any of the other adults on the outing

471
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
would have done them.

472
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
The important was they did it

473
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
for themselves.

474
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
Every once in a while,

475
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
something untoward happens,

476
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
But the scouts have elected

477
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
patrol leaders and a senior patrol leader

478
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
who are responsible for seeing that things go well.

479
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
They've learned to ask us

480
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
when they need help with these things.

481
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
You asked a question that I get asked a lot.

482
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
How do you hold scouts accountable

483
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
and responsible? Because they're doing

484
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
things for themselves. they're always busy,

485
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
So they don't disappear when

486
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
things to be done, because they're the only ones

487
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
who are going to do them.

488
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
We don't worry about making them responsible

489
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
or holding them accountable to anything,

490
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
because they're actually responsible

491
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
and accountable to themselves.

492
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
If they don't do it, it doesn't get done,

493
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
And since the program is something

494
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
that they do for themselves,

495
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
they're not waiting on some adult

496
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
to tell them what to do next.

497
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
This is a reasonably good

498
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
explanation of the patrol method.

499
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
Boys form themselves into patrols,

500
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
select their leaders. We work with

501
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
those leaders to help them discover

502
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
their abilities and talents

503
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
and get them the resources that they need,

504
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
And then we're off camping.

505
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
Now can this happen with a really young troop?

506
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
Well, yes, it certainly can.

507
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
It'll take a while to develop,

508
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
But the backpacking weekend I just described to you-

509
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
it can happen. There's no way

510
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
to apply this whole patrol method idea

511
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
a little bit. It takes

512
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
full commitment from all the adults involved

513
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
And when it comes down to the very

514
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
practical aspects and mechanics of this,

515
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
I have little idea of how my scouts do dishes.

516
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
They've been

517
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
instructed on how to do that, They have the right equipment

518
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
for it And once on a blue moon

519
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
I'll stroll through a patrol campsite and check on dishes

520
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
just to see how things are going.

521
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
And he concerns I may have

522
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
get handed to the senior patrol leader

523
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
and he's going to take them

524
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
to the patrol leader. As for

525
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
duty rosters and things

526
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
like that, it's really none of my business.

527
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
I'm pretty sure our scouts

528
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
don't use duty rosters

529
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
or plans. They just sort of all

530
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
pitch in. I honestly don't get involved

531
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
with that sort of thing. It's a scout decision.

532
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
I mean, there are general expectations

533
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
and so long as their decisions are in bounds

534
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
of those expectations. I really don't

535
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
have a whole lot of concern over the details.

536
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
So scouting is not something that

537
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
a bunch of good natured adults present

538
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
to a group of boys. This is

539
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
something that boys do for themselves

540
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
When they're actually responsible

541
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
and accountable for what they do.

542
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
you don't have to hold them responsible

543
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
and accountable. The only real limitation

544
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
is your understanding

545
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
and application of the patrol system.

546
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
Well, I hope that helps a little bit

547
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
and I really appreciate you for getting in

548
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
touch, and you're going to find out

549
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
how you can get in touch

550
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
in just a moment.

551
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
Well, thank you so much

552
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
for listening to this edition

553
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
